
%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:e2e@

%9Parameters:%9

    HELP:        A guide with the state of the current window and the list
                 of subcommands (same as /E2E without arguments).
    ON:          Turns encryption on in the current channel or query
                 (normal mode).
    OFF:         Turns encryption off; the mode is remembered. In a query
                 only for the person's current address (or, when it is
                 not known, the one this query had); other addresses of
                 that nick with encryption on are listed. With an
                 ident@host or a channel name: turns it off for that
                 address or channel, from any window.
    MODE:        Sets the mode and also turns encryption ON. Without an
                 argument: normal; "auto" = auto-accept.
                   normal       a key request waits for /E2E ACCEPT,
                                unless you accepted that person here
                                before,
                   auto-accept  the key is sent back automatically,
                   quiet        requests from people whose key you do
                                not have are dropped silently (nothing
                                of theirs is stored); the others wait
                                for /E2E ACCEPT.
    FINGERPRINT: The full fingerprint of your key (creates the key if it
                 does not exist).
    STATUS:      Fingerprint, number of incoming sessions and number of
                 channels or queries with encryption on, then where it is
                 on as <network>:<name> (old entries of no network by
                 their name alone, with a hint to /E2E ADOPT).
    LIST:        Sessions in the current channel or query: ident@host,
                 channel, start of the fingerprint, status (trusted,
                 pending, revoked, or "key held, not accepted": you can
                 read them, but they do not get your key). -all: the whole
                 keyring - known people and all sessions. Channels and
                 queries are shown as <network>:<name>. In a window also
                 the key requests waiting there ("pending: <nick>
                 (<ident@host>)").
    PENDING:     Every key request waiting for /E2E ACCEPT, on every
                 network: nick, ident@host, <network>:<name>, the start of
                 the fingerprint of the key and how long it has waited.
    HANDSHAKE:   Sends the person a key request (KEYREQ) for the current
                 channel or query; encryption must be on here and their
                 ident@host known (a common channel, a query).
    ACCEPT:      Accepts the person: sends your key back (KEYRSP) to
                 their pending request and requests theirs in return;
                 their later requests here are answered without asking.
                 Without an argument: the only request waiting in this
                 window (with several they are listed - name one). With a
                 nick: the request waiting here from that nick (compared
                 as the server compares nicks; when the nick is here now,
                 only the request of its address now); when the nick
                 names requests of two addresses, none is accepted - they
                 are listed, name one by its ident@host. With an
                 ident@host: in a channel window the channel's request of
                 that address, if one waits there; otherwise - from any
                 window of the network - the request of that address for
                 a query with it. Only requests of this window's channel
                 or query, or of that address's query, on this network
                 are ever taken. The answer goes to the nick the request
                 came under: while that nick is someone else here now,
                 the request is not accepted (it waits until that
                 address is back under a nick). Without a pending
                 request, a nick marks their session here (an ident@host:
                 their query's session) as trusted and accepts them -
                 only when a real key from them is stored. A query's key
                 goes only to that query's own address: a request from
                 anyone else for it is damaged (rpe2e.pl kept such
                 requests before) - listed by /E2E PENDING and /E2E
                 ACCEPT, never accepted, removed by /E2E DECLINE alone in
                 that query. Such an address that was accepted already (by
                 an erssi before 2026-10) is damaged too: it may hold your
                 key of the query, so that key rotates by itself with your
                 next message there (you are told once) and the new one
                 never reaches it; /E2E LIST shows it, and /E2E DECLINE
                 <ident@host> (or alone) in that query removes it.
    DECLINE:     Declines a pending exchange, chosen as for ACCEPT; an
                 existing session gets the revoked status (their messages
                 are not read, and their key is not asked for again) and
                 the person is no longer accepted: they get none of your
                 later keys. Your key is not rotated (as in repartee): if
                 they had it, they can read your messages there until it
                 changes - you are told so; /E2E ROTATE there (or REVOKE)
                 makes a new one.
    REVOKE:      Withdraws trust in the current channel or query and
                 schedules a rotation of your key, so that person can no
                 longer read along; they are no longer accepted.
    UNREVOKE:    Restores trust in their session (only when a real key
                 from them is stored); to give them your key again,
                 /E2E ACCEPT them. Where no key of theirs is stored, it
                 lifts a revocation of the person, so that their next
                 key exchange (their next message asks for it) is taken.
    FORGET:      Removes the person's sessions in the current channel or
                 query, and they are no longer accepted there; with -all
                 (at the start or at the end) - everything about them from
                 the keyring. As with DECLINE, your key is not rotated: a
                 key of yours they hold still reads your messages until
                 /E2E ROTATE (or REVOKE) there.
    VERIFY:      Shows the start of your fingerprint and of the person's -
                 to compare over another channel (e.g. by phone).
    REVERIFY:    Accepts a trust change that blocks the person's key
                 exchange - you are told of it in the window (and their
                 messages say so), /E2E STATUS, LIST and PENDING list
                 what waits. A known key under a new ident@host (they
                 came back from another host): the key is moved to the
                 new address, with the channels it was accepted for. A
                 new key at a known address (a reinstall - or someone
                 else: compare fingerprints first): the new key replaces
                 the old one. Name the new ident@host, the nick (only a
                 key you trust that is known as that nick counts - not who
                 holds the nick now - and a nick known at several
                 addresses is refused), or the old address; when more
                 than one change waits, nothing is guessed - they are
                 listed, and a second argument, at least 8 hex digits of
                 the fingerprint, picks one. Then their key is
                 asked for again (KEYREQ) where encryption is on, so their
                 next message is read. Without a recorded change it clears
                 the old data of that address and a new exchange is
                 needed. A revoked person is answered by UNREVOKE.
    ROTATE:      Schedules a rotation of your key in the current channel
                 or query; the new key is created with the next message and
                 sent to trusted people.
    EXPORT:      Writes the whole keyring, including private keys, to a
                 new JSON file (mode 0600); an existing file or a symlink
                 is never overwritten.
    IMPORT:      REPLACES the whole keyring with the file's contents (no
                 merging) - an export, or a keyring file itself such as a
                 keyring.json.bak-<time> (checked as the keyring is, and
                 refused without a whole identity - EXPORT refuses that
                 too); the previous one is kept as keyring.json.bak-<time>.
                 Only a regular file is read: a symlink is not followed,
                 a FIFO, device or directory is refused at once.
                 The key changes (REKEY) already taken are part of
                 erssi's exports; an export without them (repartee's)
                 starts their replay protection anew - an old key change
                 recorded by someone could then be taken once more.
    AUTOTRUST:   Without an argument or with LIST - the list of rules. ADD
                 adds a rule: the scope is "global" or an exact channel
                 name (or "@ident@host" of a query), the pattern is
                 ident@host with * and ? (case-insensitive); matching
                 people are accepted automatically. A channel added here
                 is the one of the window's network (listed as
                 <network>:<name>; a window without a server cannot add
                 one); rules from rpe2e.pl or from erssi
                 before networks were kept apart name a channel of every
                 network (listed with "(every network)"). REMOVE removes
                 the rules with that pattern.
    ADOPT:       Binds the old entries of the current channel or query
                 (from rpe2e.pl or from erssi before networks were kept
                 apart) to the window's network; in a query also the
                 person's keys for their messages to you. Needed when
                 erssi uses several networks: until then E2E there is
                 refused. When another network took the old entries
                 over already, they stay there and this network gets
                 encryption of its own - always turned on, in normal
                 mode (keys are exchanged anew; /E2E OFF afterwards
                 sends in clear text there). When the network has
                 entries of its own for the name, nothing changes (to
                 use the old ones there instead: /E2E EXPORT, remove
                 that network's entries for the name from the file,
                 /E2E IMPORT it, then /E2E ADOPT).
    RESET:       After a corrupt keyring was moved aside (see below):
                 starts a new keyring with a new identity. Encryption is
                 then off everywhere until /E2E ON, and your peers see a
                 new key: an erssi user runs /E2E REVERIFY <your nick>, a
                 repartee or rpe2e.pl user /E2E REVERIFY <your nick> and
                 then /E2E HANDSHAKE <your nick>; you /E2E ACCEPT their
                 requests then (the new keyring has accepted nobody).
                 On a keyring that is fine, only with -force (to start
                 over on purpose); the old one is kept as
                 keyring.json.bak-<time> (/E2E IMPORT it to go back).

%9Description:%9

    RPE2E end-to-end encryption (XChaCha20-Poly1305, a separate key per
    sender and channel or query), compatible with repartee, the rpe2e.pl
    script and the WeeChat rpe2e.py script. Built into erssi (the e2e
    module, loaded at start through the autoload_modules setting; /load e2e
    loads it by hand - in lower case, as the module's file name is built
    from the word as typed). Taken out of autoload_modules, it is not
    loaded again after a restart or /UPGRADE, and messages to channels and
    queries with E2E on are then sent in clear text; the module says so
    when it is loaded and at /E2E ON (with the /SET to run), and at
    /UPGRADE while E2E is on anywhere (printed just before the restart,
    so it may be gone from the screen afterwards; a log of that window
    keeps it). /unload e2e warns while E2E is on anywhere or the keyring
    is lost. Keys are stored in ~/.erssi/rpe2e/keyring.json - the file
    rpe2e.pl uses, so moving from the script needs no new keys.

    If the rpe2e.pl script is loaded too, the module stays inactive and
    says so; unload the script (/SCRIPT UNLOAD rpe2e, and remove it from
    scripts/autorun) to use the module. The script does not know the
    module's per-network entries (see Networks), so it would send a
    channel or query that only the module set up in clear text: while the
    script is loaded the module still stops such messages (they are NOT
    sent, and you are told), lets bot commands out with a warning only
    while e2e_bot_bypass is on, and warns once that the keyring has such
    entries. Going back to rpe2e.pl after the module wrote them is a
    downgrade - unload the script to send there encrypted; /E2E EXPORT
    keeps a copy.

    Subcommands work in the context of the active window: in a channel
    window it is the channel, in a query window - the person identified by
    ident@host. Encryption in a query requires the other side's ident@host
    to be known (an open query, a common channel or an earlier exchange)
    and your own, which is learned via WHOIS after connecting - until then
    some commands ask you to retry. A nick in ACCEPT and DECLINE names a
    request waiting in the window first; nicks in REVOKE, VERIFY etc. are
    resolved using the data stored in the keyring.

    Once enabled, every PRIVMSG to the channel or query (plain text, /ME,
    /MSG, /SAY, /QUOTE PRIVMSG in any case, scripts, the web client) is
    encrypted. When encryption is not possible, the message is NOT sent,
    although it is shown locally, and an [E2E] message is printed; a line
    to several targets at once (#a,#b) is not sent when one of them is
    encrypted. Exceptions: CTCPs other than ACTION are always sent in
    plain text, and NOTICEs are not encrypted. With /SET e2e_bot_bypass
    ON (off by default, as in repartee; rpe2e.pl always did it), lines on
    a channel starting with '.' or '!' followed by a letter (bot commands
    such as .op or !seen) are sent in plain text, with a warning - "..."
    or "!!" are encrypted all the same; with it off they are encrypted
    like any other line. A keyring that cannot be read, or is lost, lets
    no such line out either.

    An encrypted message that cannot be read is never dropped without a
    word: in its place - as a message of its sender, logged like one -
    comes a line in brackets that says why and what to do: "[E2E:
    encrypted message not read - no key from <nick> (<ident@host>) yet -
    key requested]" when there is no session with them yet (a key request
    goes to them at most every 30 s for each channel or query, at most 3
    in 10 s to everyone together - people you confirmed (/E2E ACCEPT, an
    AUTOTRUST rule, /E2E REVERIFY, /E2E UNREVOKE, an exchange you started
    with /E2E HANDSHAKE; and, once, every key trusted in a keyring of an
    erssi before 2026.10.3 or of rpe2e.pl, when this version first reads
    it; an import keeps what the export says) are not held back by that -
    and only while encryption is on here; their answer lets you read
    their next messages), "[E2E rejected: ...]" in red when it does not
    open with their key (their current key is then requested - their
    REKEY may not have reached you) or you declined or revoked them here
    (then nothing is requested), "too old to verify" when its time is
    more than 5 minutes off (history a bouncer plays back - your own
    lines too - a replay, or a clock far off), and the reason when a key
    exchange of theirs is blocked (a new address, a new key, revoked -
    see REVERIFY); such a block is also told in full, with the command
    to run, when it happens and at most once a minute while they write.

    Your own key goes only to people you accepted: /E2E ACCEPT, an
    AUTOTRUST rule or auto-accept mode. Having their key is not enough -
    so in normal mode each of you accepts the other once. A request for a
    channel is announced in its window as "Pending key exchange from
    <nick> (<ident@host>) for <network>:<name>" - reply there with /E2E
    ACCEPT or /E2E DECLINE (alone, or with the ident@host shown); one for
    a query as "... for a query", answered with /E2E ACCEPT <ident@host>
    from any window of that network. Asked again, a request is announced
    at most every 5 minutes, and /E2E PENDING lists every request that
    waits. Later requests from an accepted person are answered at once.
    Requests for a channel or query where encryption is off are ignored, a
    request for a query must come from the person it names, and each kind
    of request (KEYREQ, KEYRSP, REKEY) from one person for one context is
    handled at most every 10 s; requests from people you have not
    confirmed that would be answered or stored are taken at most 5 in 10 s
    together, and at most 10 key exchange messages leave in 10 s in all.
    Those to people you have not confirmed never wait in irssi's send
    queue: they wait in the module (at most 8; the oldest goes when
    another comes, "many key requests at once - some are ignored"), and
    leave one at a time only while nothing else waits to be sent - your
    lines, their parts, key changes and answers to people you confirmed
    never wait behind them. A key change announced by a person (REKEY)
    only replaces a key you already have from them (the key it replaces
    still opens their messages for 2 minutes, for those sent just before
    it), and each one is taken once (after 1024 of them from one person in
    one context the next key change goes through a new key request
    instead). When your own key changes, only people you accepted get the
    new one. At most 64 requests wait for /E2E ACCEPT (each for a day at
    most), at most 256 of your own key requests wait for an answer and at
    most 256 people you never accepted are remembered, with their keys;
    the oldest go first (people you accepted are always kept). Messages of
    the module are prefixed with [E2E] (warnings in the error colour);
    with /SET e2e_debug ON it also traces the key exchange. Line breaks in
    a decrypted message are shown as spaces.

    A message to a nick is not sent when encryption is on for that nick
    at another ident@host (a new host, or someone else took the nick), or
    when its address is not known at the moment (no query, no common
    channel): /E2E ON in the query encrypts to the new address after a
    new key exchange, /E2E OFF <ident@host> turns encryption off for the
    old one.
    A raw line holding several IRC lines (CR/LF, from a script) is not
    sent when one of them is a PRIVMSG to an encrypted conversation, and
    an encrypted line that would be longer than the server allows (a very
    long channel name) is not sent either. /UPGRADE sends the messages
    still waiting in the flood queue through the encryption as well.

    Long messages: an encrypted message is sent in parts (chunks) of up to
    180 bytes, each a line of its own, and they leave through the flood
    queue like other commands (cmds_max_at_once, cmd_queue_speed; irssi
    also waits 2 s and 1 s per 100 bytes after a long line), so a message
    of several parts can take some seconds to go out - a line you type is
    split by irssi at the server's line limit first, and each of those
    lines is encrypted on its own. Later messages to that network wait
    behind the parts and leave in the order typed; each is encrypted when
    its turn comes, so after /E2E OFF the messages still waiting go out in
    clear text. Other commands (PART, NICK, QUIT, TOPIC, NOTICEs) do not
    wait: they leave before the parts still waiting - after /PART the rest
    of a message to a +n channel is rejected by the server. On disconnect
    or /E2E RESET the parts not sent yet are dropped and the number is
    shown in the status window (the receiver gets an incomplete message).
    /unload e2e drops them too, and the messages in irssi's own flood
    queue that the module would encrypt or refuse (they would leave in
    clear text without it); clear text it would let pass stays queued.
    /UPGRADE sends everything waiting at once, past the flood limit,
    before it restarts. When rpe2e.pl is loaded the script encrypts, and
    what still waits in the module is dropped the same way.

    Statusbar: the e2e item shows the E2E state of the active channel or
    query - "e2e off"; "E2E" (on, a key from someone to read); "E2E
    waiting for keys" (on, no key from anyone yet); "E2E blocked" (not
    sent: an old entry of unknown network, or the query's nick has E2E
    on at another address); "E2E keyring error" - followed by "N to
    accept" while N key requests wait there for /E2E ACCEPT. Nothing in
    other windows, and nothing while rpe2e.pl is loaded. The shipped
    config has it in the info bar, after "window"; a config file that
    holds the info bar exactly as an older erssi shipped it gets the
    item there once, at the first start. A config whose bars have item
    lists of their own (changed by /STATUSBAR ADDITEM, REMOVEITEM, or
    by hand) keeps them as they are - add it with
    /STATUSBAR ADDITEM -after window e2e <bar> (the shipped bar is
    "info"). The texts are the formats e2e_sb_off, e2e_sb_on,
    e2e_sb_waiting, e2e_sb_blocked, e2e_sb_error and e2e_sb_pending
    (/FORMAT, themes). A keyring changed by another program (rpe2e.pl in
    another erssi) is shown at the next window switch or the next
    message sent.

    Web clients (fe-web, see /HELP FE_WEB) see the same state of every
    channel and query and can accept or decline a key request that waits
    there - only that request, by its exact ident@host.

    Keyrings: a keyring that cannot be read is never overwritten, and
    messages are not sent until it can be read again - so is one that is
    not a regular file of yours (keyring.json as a symlink, which is not
    followed: link the rpe2e directory instead; a FIFO, a device). The
    subcommands (but HELP and IMPORT) refuse then, so that EXPORT never
    writes an empty export and nothing claims a change that was not
    saved. One that is not
    valid JSON is moved aside to keyring.json.corrupt-<time>; the E2E
    state is then LOST: nothing is sent to any channel or query and
    nothing is saved, also after a restart, until you run /E2E RESET (a
    new keyring) or /E2E IMPORT a keyring export. Which people you
    accepted is stored in keyring.json as "accepted" (rpe2e.pl keeps it,
    exports do not carry it): with a keyring from rpe2e.pl, repartee or an
    import, a person asking for your key again needs one /E2E ACCEPT, and
    does not get your new key when it rotates before that.

    Channel names are compared as the server's CASEMAPPING compares
    them: ascii, rfc1459 and strict-rfc1459 exactly (rfc1459 when the
    server names none), and Latin-1 letters as servers of the ircu
    family fold them; rfc7613, rfc8265 and utf8 with a wider fold (NFKC,
    case folding and the rfc1459 specials), which may refuse a message
    to a channel the server keeps apart, but never lets one out in clear
    text to a channel with E2E on. Until the server has named its
    CASEMAPPING, or ended its MOTD without one, the wider fold is used.
    A name the wider fold cannot decide - not valid UTF-8 or holding an
    unassigned character on such a server, and any non-ASCII name on a
    server whose CASEMAPPING erssi does not know (it may be a byte code
    page such as cp1251) - is not sent in clear text while E2E is on for
    any channel of that network (and while E2E is on for such a name,
    other names there that cannot be told apart from it get no clear
    text either). On IRCnet, !name is refused while E2E is on for a
    !XXXXXname of that name. The nick of a query with E2E on, also one
    rpe2e.pl keeps by nick, is compared the same way.

    A message of a peer that opens only under the key of a channel the
    wider fold takes for this one, though some servers keep the two
    apart (for example #a[b and #a{b), is shown with "(E2E key of
    #a[b)": the server may have moved it from that channel.

    What end-to-end encryption protects, and what it does not:

    Protected: the text of PRIVMSGs and actions (/ME) in a channel or
    query with encryption on, against the server and the other users:
    nobody without the keys can read it or change it unnoticed. Once you
    verified a person's key (/E2E VERIFY, compared over another channel),
    the keys you get from them are really theirs.

    Not protected:
      - NOTICEs, CTCPs other than ACTION, and bot commands while
        e2e_bot_bypass is on: they are sent in clear text;
      - nicks, addresses, channel names, who talks to whom, when, and
        how long the messages are (the server sees all of it);
      - who of a channel's readers wrote a line: a sender's key there
        is shared with everyone they let read, so one of those, with
        the server's help, could pass a line off as the sender's;
      - what is on your own machine: messages are decrypted locally, so
        logs, the Mentions window, terminal notifications and web
        clients (fe-web) get them in clear text;
      - lines that arrive in clear text - from someone without E2E, or
        put there by a hostile server under any nick. They are shown
        with "[plain]" in red before the text, so that they do not look
        decrypted (/SET e2e_mark_plain OFF turns that off; your own
        lines are never marked).

    No forward secrecy: the session keys stay in keyring.json while they
    are used, and a new key (a rotation, REKEY) is sent wrapped to the
    recipient's long-term identity key, as in repartee (RPE2E v1.0).
    Whoever recorded the traffic and later gets a recipient's identity
    private key (or keyring.json) can read those sessions - rotating keys
    does not undo that. Keep keyring.json private (erssi writes it with
    mode 0600, its exports too); after a suspected compromise run
    /E2E RESET -force (a new identity), remove the old
    keyring.json.bak-<time> copies, and have people verify your new key.

    Replays: a message the server delivers again is dropped while its
    timestamp is still taken (5 minutes either way), also after a
    restart; each sender has room for its last 512 parts in each channel
    or query, so a flood can make only the flooder's own older lines
    repeatable (details under "Known limitations" below).

    Forward secrecy, encrypted NOTICEs or signed lines would need a new
    version of the protocol, shared with repartee and its scripts; erssi
    keeps the wire format of RPE2E v1.0, so that they all understand it.

    Known limitations: a server that names rfc1459, ascii or
    strict-rfc1459 but folds the bytes of another code page (koi8-r,
    cp1251) is not covered: such names may be one channel to it and two to
    erssi. NOTICEs, CTCPs other than ACTION, bot commands with
    e2e_bot_bypass on and IRCv3 message tags are never encrypted. Nicks in
    ACCEPT, REVOKE, VERIFY etc. are resolved with the last nick stored in
    the keyring, which may meanwhile belong to someone else - check the
    ident@host the command prints. A keyring written with real Unicode
    (repartee's export) that stores a nick whose letters all fit Latin-1
    (kótek) is read as Latin-1 bytes, so neither erssi nor rpe2e.pl finds
    that nick by it (one with other letters, żbik, is found). As in
    repartee, the server can drop or reorder the parts of a long message
    (each part is shown on its own). A part read before that the server
    delivers again - in any spelling - is dropped while its timestamp is
    still taken (5 minutes either way), with "repeated message from <nick>
    dropped" (once a minute per sender). Only parts that opened are
    remembered, the last 512 of each sender in each channel or query: a
    sender's flood pushes out only that sender's own older parts, never
    anyone else's (16384 are kept in all; past that, the sender holding
    the most loses its oldest). They are kept in rpe2e/replay.cache next
    to the keyring - digests and times only, no text, names or keys -
    saved every 10 s when something changed, at /UPGRADE and when erssi
    exits, so a restart does not let a repeat through (after a crash the
    last 10 s may be missing). A cache file that cannot be read (damaged,
    too large, not a regular file of yours) is not used: the cache starts
    empty, with a warning, and the file is replaced at the next save;
    nothing else depends on it. A key request carries no ident@host:
    someone who forwards your peer's request before the peer's first
    contact with you ties that key to their own address, and the peer's
    own request is then held back as a changed address until /E2E REVERIFY
    (nobody gets a key that way). The rpe2e.pl script is recognised by its
    file name (rpe2e*.pl).

    Networks: keys, sessions and settings are kept per network - the
    chatnet name of the server, otherwise its tag - so #test on one
    network and #test on another are two conversations with keys of
    their own (in keyring.json as "<network>" U+001F "#test"; on the wire
    only the bare name is used, as rpe2e.pl and repartee do). A keyring
    from rpe2e.pl or from erssi before this (one entry per name, for any
    network) is used as it is while erssi uses one network (counting the
    servers connected, connecting, waiting to reconnect and set to
    autoconnect) - nothing to do - and becomes that network's with the
    first message you send there (or /E2E ADOPT) - messages and key
    requests from others never move it.
    In a query, your first message to the person also takes along the
    keys of their messages to you; those of other people stay old until
    you write to them while erssi uses one network - on several networks
    their messages are not read until /E2E ADOPT.
    With several networks erssi cannot know which network an old entry
    belongs to: a message to such a channel or query is NOT sent (you are
    told why), and nothing is decrypted or answered for it there, until
    /E2E ADOPT in its window binds the old entries to that network, or
    /E2E OFF turns encryption off for it on that network. Once old entries
    belong to one network, the same name on any other network is refused
    the same way until it is set up there (/E2E ADOPT, /E2E OFF); the
    entries of one network never move to another. EXPORT and IMPORT keep
    the per-network entries as they are (repartee's exports use the same
    form).
    A network without a chatnet is labelled by its server tag, which can
    change when tags collide - set a chatnet for stable E2E scopes
    (/E2E ON warns on such a server). So that a changed label never
    sends in clear text where E2E was on: on a server without a chatnet,
    and wherever the label differs from another one only in case (a
    chatnet added for a tag), a message to a channel with no E2E entry
    of its own there is NOT sent while E2E is on for that channel under
    the other label - /E2E ON or /E2E OFF in its window settles it.
    A channel or address whose name holds the control character U+001F
    cannot be kept per network: nothing is encrypted for it, and a
    message to it is not sent while an old entry may have encryption on.

    Decrypted private messages and hilights are plain text again in the
    client: they go to the Mentions window and the mentions log, and with
    term_notify_text ON into terminal notifications. Use /SET
    term_notify_text OFF (and /SET mentions_log OFF) if that is not wanted.

    EXPORT and IMPORT do not expand '~' - give a full path.

%9Examples:%9

    /E2E
    /E2E ON
    /E2E PENDING
    /E2E ACCEPT
    /E2E ACCEPT alice
    /E2E DECLINE mallory@host.example.org
    /E2E VERIFY alice
    /E2E REVERIFY alice@new.example.org
    /E2E MODE auto-accept
    /E2E LIST -all
    /E2E FORGET -all bob
    /E2E AUTOTRUST ADD global *@*.example.org
    /E2E ADOPT
    /E2E EXPORT /path/to/e2e-keyring.json
    /E2E RESET
    /E2E OFF bob@old.example.org

%9See also:%9 MSG, QUERY, LOAD, STATUSBAR, FORMAT, FE_WEB

